        84.99369812011719,
        522.855224609375,
        203.03359985351562
      ],
      "text": "survey conducted by the American Psychological Association, is related to\nstress. In other words, a pre-programmed physiological reaction, which\nevolution has equipped us with to help us avoid famished predators, is now\nbeing triggered by an online post. Social media posting, according to one study,\nraises your pulse, makes you sweat, and enlarges your pupils more than most\ndaily activities.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        248.07469177246094,
        517.6565551757812,
        465.4736022949219
      ],
      "text": "The fact that stress increases the likelihood that we will focus more on\nalarming messages, together with the fact that it spreads extremely rapidly,\ncan create collective fear that is not always justified. After a stressful public\nevent, such as a natural disaster or major financial crash, there is often a wave\nof alarming information in traditional and social media, which individuals\nbecome very aware of. But that has the effect of exaggerating existing danger.\nAnd so, a reliable pattern emerges - stress is triggered, spreading from one\nperson to the next, which temporarily enhances the likelihood that people will\ntake in negative reports, which increases stress further. As a result, trips are\ncancelled, even if the disaster took place across the globe; stocks are sold,\neven when holding on is the best thing to do.\n"
    },
    {
      "box": [
        72.0,
        510.3937072753906,
        504.1743469238281,
        608.6336059570312
      ],
      "text": "The good news, however, is that positive emotions, such as hope, are\ncontagious too, and are powerful in inducing people to act to find solutions.\nBeing aware of the close relationship between people's emotional state and\nhow they process information can help us frame our messages more\neffectively and become conscientious agents of change.\n"
    },
